Yes. **`labwc-network-scan-action-root.tmpl` is a good Perl migration candidate**, and I would migrate the privileged root helper together with a dedicated Perl module set.

I would **not** necessarily migrate the unprivileged `labwc-network-scan-action` at the same time. Keeping that wrapper in shell while moving the root implementation is a reasonable staged boundary.

## Why the root helper is a good Perl candidate

The root template contains substantial policy and operational logic:

- pkexec caller validation;
- managed-user validation;
- private versus authorized-WAN target policy;
- IPv4 parsing and classification;
- reserved/public/private range rejection;
- interface validation;
- Nmap script allowlisting;
- approved TCP/UDP port policy;
- bounded scan execution;
- multiple scan profiles;
- tcpdump capture handling;
- timeout and rate-limit policy;
- privileged command execution;
- output/error propagation.

That is much more than a shell wrapper. It has a real domain model:

```text
network scan request
target scope
authorization class
tool/action
timeout policy
rate limit
allowlisted scripts
capture interface
```

Perl can express that more clearly than nested shell functions and `awk` pipelines.

## Recommended layout

Keep the public path unchanged:

```text
/usr/local/libexec/labwc-network-scan-action-root
```

Keep the installer source as a template because it contains:

```text
__INSTALLER_ACCOUNT_USERNAME__
```

Then stage the Perl modules under one private module root:

```text
/usr/local/libexec/labwc-network-scan/
    NetworkScan/
        CLI.pm
        Config.pm
        Validation.pm
        TargetPolicy.pm
        Interface.pm
        Command.pm
        Nmap.pm
        Capture.pm
        Runtime.pm
        Logger.pm
```

The template itself can become a thin Perl entry point:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;

use lib '/usr/local/libexec/labwc-network-scan';
use NetworkScan::CLI;

exit NetworkScan::CLI->new(
    managed_user => '__INSTALLER_ACCOUNT_USERNAME__',
)->run(@ARGV);
```

The staging renderer should replace the placeholder with the validated installer username as it does today.

## Suggested responsibilities

### `NetworkScan::CLI`

Own:

- action names;
- argument counts;
- dispatch;
- exit-code behavior;
- user-facing error messages.

### `NetworkScan::Validation`

Own:

- IPv4 syntax;
- private/loopback checks;
- reserved/documentation/multicast range rejection;
- prefix-length constraints;
- interface names;
- action arguments.

### `NetworkScan::TargetPolicy`

Own:

- `private-scan`;
- `authorized-wan-scan`;
- maximum target size;
- single-host requirements;
- per-scope rate limits.

This is worth separating because target authorization is a security policy, not merely parsing.

### `NetworkScan::Command`

Own explicit argv execution:

```perl
system { $command } $command, @args;
```

Do not use shell strings or interpolate target values into command text.

### `NetworkScan::Nmap`

Own:

- scan profile definitions;
- required NSE scripts;
- allowed script paths;
- timeout;
- retry;
- rate;
- host-timeout;
- script-timeout options.

### `NetworkScan::Capture`

Own:

- tcpdump;
- interface checks;
- capture limits;
- output handling;
- capture-specific timeout and cleanup behavior.

### `NetworkScan::Runtime`

Own:

- temporary paths;
- cleanup;
- signals;
- output file lifecycle;
- common command result handling.

## Moo/MooX recommendation

Use Moo selectively:

```perl
use Moo;
use MooX::StrictConstructor;
use MooX::TypeTiny;
use Types::Standard qw(Bool Int Str);
```

Good object boundaries:

- `NetworkScan::CLI`;
- `NetworkScan::TargetPolicy`;
- `NetworkScan::Command`;
- `NetworkScan::Nmap`;
- `NetworkScan::Capture`;
- `NetworkScan::Runtime`.

Keep pure validators as plain functions where possible.

I would not add `MooX::Options` unless you intentionally redesign the command-line interface. The current action protocol is positional and should remain stable.

## What should remain shell initially

Keep:

```text
/usr/local/bin/labwc-network-scan-action
```

as the unprivileged shell wrapper initially.

That wrapper already provides a useful trust boundary:

```text
desktop user
  -> shell action wrapper
  -> pkexec
  -> Perl privileged root helper
```

It can continue doing basic validation and launch the root helper, but the Perl root helper must repeat all security-relevant validation independently.

## Important security requirements

The Perl rewrite must preserve:

- non-root caller enforcement through pkexec;
- exact managed-user restriction;
- private versus authorized-WAN distinction;
- refusal of non-public WAN targets;
- prefix-length limits;
- Nmap script allowlisting;
- command timeouts;
- scan rate limits;
- absolute executable paths or controlled `PATH`;
- AppArmor executable path contracts;
- generated template behavior;
- exact exit-status semantics.

The root helper must never trust a validation result produced by the unprivileged shell wrapper.

## Should the `.tmpl` remain a template?

Yes. Keep:

```text
labwc-network-scan-action-root.tmpl
```

as the authoritative source if the installer username is substituted during staging.

The template should contain only the small Perl entrypoint and the placeholder. The reusable implementation should live in the staged module tree.

That is preferable to embedding hundreds of lines of Perl directly in the template.

## Final recommendation

**Yes, migrate the root template to Perl.**

Recommended first phase:

```text
labwc-network-scan-action-root.tmpl  -> Perl entrypoint
NetworkScan::*                       -> Perl modules
labwc-network-scan-action            -> remain shell
```

Then, after the root helper is stable, decide whether the unprivileged wrapper should also migrate. The root helper is the stronger candidate because it contains the real policy, validation, and privileged operation logic. No files were changed and no tests were run.

